I was away at camp all last week and we sent you video letters from summer camp.

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
And, man, we got a fantastic response with dozens of email messages and questions

10
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
And those are all in the videos

11
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
And I came back home to a full mailbag. so let's get into that real quick.

12
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:39,000
We heard from Tony Lucaba, who is an assistant scoutmaster with Truth 581 in Bristow, Virginia.

13
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Tony said I really enjoyed your letters from summer camp.

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
As usual, your messages are informative and entertaining. Thanks for all your hard work.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Our scoutmaster sends email updates once or twice during the week when our troop is off to summer camp.

16
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,000
I'm thinking with today's newfangled technology we could step into the present and do our own video message to provide updates to parents.

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Hey, Tony, that sounds like a really good idea.

18
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Ella Marie Kazell is with Troop 1 in Akron, Ohio. We just returned from our week at beautiful camp Manitoc in Peninsula Ohio.

19
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
I like seeing how you do things at Camp Horseshoe. Can you show us a campsite?

20
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Well, I'll tell you what, Ella Marie. go to any one of last week's posts with the letters from summer camp.

21
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
You'll find a link to the Horseshoe Scout Reservation website

22
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
And they'll do a lot better job of telling you about the sites and all that than I can.

23
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Your dining hall looks massive. How many do you have at camp?

24
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Well, there's about anywhere between 250 and 300 scouts and you know 80 to 150 adults at any given week.

25
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
There's a full eight week season.

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Love the letters from camp makes me miss being there. Thanks so much for getting in touch,

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Ella Marie. I really do appreciate it.

28
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Atik Riemann got in touch and said hi and greetings from Forest Law and Scout Reservation in Cedar Glen, California.

29
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
I'm here participating in summer camps as an international camp staff member from Pakistan

30
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
And I hear the Scoutmaster podcast, Forest Law and Scout Reservation radio.

31
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Well, that's exactly right And that's being played during the summer there

32
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
And I really appreciate you getting in touch with me. Atik

33
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Liz wrote to say aloha. I had to say hi. when I saw the live chat button,

34
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
I discovered your webpage and blog this past year.

35
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
I've been a scouter for 10 years. Thank you for keeping up the flame. Mahalo,

36
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Well, Mahalo to you too, Liz, and thanks for getting in touch.

37
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:34,000
If I'm around and I'm working on the site, I will turn on the live chat feature and we can have a live chat

38
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
If I'm not there. well, the chat button always works and you can send me a quick message that way.

39
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
But I'm always glad to hear from you and having people get in touch.

40
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,000
As noted earlier, we were summer camp last week with the troop. in a week from today,

41
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Actually, my wife and I get on a plane to Kondersteg in the Burner Oberland of Switzerland and we have an actual vacation.

42
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
That's right. So next week is my vacation week

43
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:13,000
And then the week following we're going to be back hard at work doing what Scouts do, because I will be meeting with a group of our Scouts who will be traveling over to Kondersteg

44
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,000
And we're going to be staying at Kondersteg International Scout Center there in Switzerland

45
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
And I'll be sending dispatches related to our experience, kind of like the letters I sent from summer camp.

46
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
That's not next week, but the following,

47
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
And I hope you find those interesting.

48
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Kondersteg is just a beautiful place and I can't wait to share it with you,

49
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
So I'm happy to get in the podcast. this week.

50
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
I've got lots of email questions to answer,

51
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
But before we do that, people ask: how do we make all this happen?

52
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
As you heard at the top of the podcast, we have backer.

53
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
If you find what we're offering is helping you in your work as a scouter,

54
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:56,000
you can return the favor by joining the growing number of scouts who financially support the creation of this and all the other resources associated with ScoutmasterCGcom and become a backer.

55
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Go to ScoutmasterCGcom, click on the support link at the top of the page and learn how you can help.

56
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And I want to take a moment to specifically thank Tom Kepley and Brad Ayers for becoming backers since our last podcast, since podcast 230..

57
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Well, as I said, I've got a ton of email questions to answer and that's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

58
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
So let's get started. shall we

59
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Come swing along to a hiking song on the highway, winding west,

60
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Tramping highland plains and tracking bends to grip the aisles

61
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:45,000
We love the best?

62
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Find me a letter, send it by name.

63
00:05:51,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Email, that is folks. And here's an answer to one of your emails.

64
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Keith Kingery is the Scoutmaster of Troop 2 in Picayune, Mississippi,

65
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
And he wrote in to say: I really enjoy the podcast and the blog.

66
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Thanks for the time and dedication that you put into it.

67
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:23,000
I'd like to hear your reflections on Baden Powell's thinking about ought to understand what it's like to be a scout.

68
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:32,000
I recall him saying things of finding the joy in the same old mud puddle as the young scout and being able to enter into their dreams and ambitions.

69
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
How do you think that we do that today?

70
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Well, Keith, well, I don't seek out friendship with my scouts.

71
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
I do enjoy being one of their friends,

72
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:47,000
And there's really a difference between striving to become one of their friends and simply being welcomed into that circle of friends.

73
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,000
I'm not one of them. I'm an adult, I'm 54 years old.

74
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,000
In their lives I have a very specific purpose. I'm their Scoutmaster.

75
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Now I get the opportunity to join in their fun from time to time.

76
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
I just welcome those opportunities when they present themselves.

77
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Now, I might not share their taste in popular culture and, truth be told,

78
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
I do my best to take as little notice of it as possible.

79
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:18,000
I'm careful not to complain about their enthusiasm in music or the way that they dress or other similar passing fads,

80
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,000
because you know what? another new one is going to come along soon

81
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:31,000
And it seems to be the birthright of every generation to drive the subsequent generation of their parents absolutely up a wall with the music that they like

82
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
and the way that they dress and the way that they talk.

83
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
As long as it's not totally inappropriate and outside of the Scout Oath and Law,

84
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
I turn as much of a blind eye as possible to it.

85
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
I think first and foremost our scouts need us to be ourselves.

86
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
They need adults who can see things from their perspective.

87
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,000
but they don't need another peer level friend, because they have plenty of those.

88
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,000
They need an adult who understands what it's like to be their age and what it's like to be a Scout

89
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
and is willing to help them and give them encouragement.

90
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Frank is a Scoutmaster and he wrote in to say:

91
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
I recently completed my first summer camp as Scoutmaster.

92
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Do you feel that an assistant Scoutmaster or any adult leader should be assigned full-time to shadow first-year campers for the entire week they're at camp?

93
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:20,000
We seem to return home with a lot of incomplete merit badges with our first-year campers than in previous years.

94
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Thanks for what you do for scouting in Scoutmasters. I really enjoy your site.

95
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Well, Frank. I really feel that adults should never be shadowing scouts in any situation.

96
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:34,000
They should be observing, perhaps giving a little bit of guidance here and there,

97
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
but as so far as making things like that happen and resolving those difficulties,

98
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:42,000
they have patrol leaders and a senior patrol leader to do that.

99
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:47,000
And I do know what you mean about managing merit badges and all that business at camp

100
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
and it does seem that an adult would be the most appropriate person to do that.

101
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:58,000
but you know what I've had? very good success when all of those responsibilities are placed squarely in the hand of the patrol leaders

102
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
and the senior patrol leader. We'll help them figure out the particulars,

103
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:09,000
but from the get-go it's clear that they are responsible to help the younger Scouts advance and to complete merit badges and things.

104
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Andy McDonald is the Scoutmaster of Troop 196 in Winter Springs, Florida.

105
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Our newer Scouts that crossed over this year have what seems to be extreme separation anxiety.

106
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
I'm not talking about your normal kind of homesickness on day three of summer camp.

107
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:31,000
I'm talking about crying Jags for no apparent reason at a one or two night monthly camp out.

108
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
I guess I'm quick to blame the parents for this phenomenon.

109
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:39,000
However, have you seen this kind of thing? What do you suggest is a possible solution?

110
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Well, Andy, what you describe is kind of a new one on me.

111
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
It may not be the parents fault altogether and parents may have nothing to do with it.

112
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Different groups of Scouts: I've seen different groups of Scouts influence each other in a multitude of different ways

113
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:58,000
And maybe this kind of crying thing is something that they cause in each other.

114
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Now, from what I understand and what you've written, they've been on several camping trips where this happens.

115
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
So they're still going camping, right?

116
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:11,000
I mean, that simple fact tells me this is something that I wouldn't particularly worry about or pay much attention to.

117
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
The Scouts will likely resolve it themselves at some point.

118
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Now, if this happened once and they never went back camping, well, that's a problem I'd need to think about.

119
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
But if it's something that happens routinely, I don't know that I'd pay it much, if any attention

120
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Indeed. the attention that you're paying to it might be one reason that it keeps going on.

121
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
That is a new one on me.

122
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
That's the best advice I can come up with to handle it.

123
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:40,000
I heard from Gretel, who said: I have some argument amongst Assistant Scoutmasters in the committee

124
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:45,000
as to what the six month terms for the Patrol Leaders Council should be.

125
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Have you any advice?

126
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Well, has anyone asked the Scouts what they would prefer to do about this?

127
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:56,000
I've found they usually have much better answers than adults- and it is their troop after all.

128
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:01,000
So I would go to them and present the conundrum to them and ask them what they think a good idea would be,

129
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
And they'll probably come up with an excellent answer.

130
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:09,000
Mike Horrocks is with Troop and Crew 215 in Carmel, Indiana,

131
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
And he writes in to say thanks for putting all of this effort together.

132
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
I'd love listening to the podcast.

133
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:19,000
One of my co-workers, who is another listener during our scouting discussions, asked what would Clark do?

134
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
But now I've got a question for you.

135
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:26,000
For years I've been a Marriott Badge Counselor and I've helped a lot of Scouts earn Marriott Badges.

136
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
Now I have sons who are in a scout troop

137
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And while I continue to help other Scouts earn the Marriott Badges,

138
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,000
I feel that it would be a conflict to counsel these badges for my own son.

139
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
I'm afraid that at a border review somewhere

140
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,000
someone will ask about that and they'll feel like I may have given them to them.

141
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:49,000
And while there is nothing against policy on my counseling my sons that I'm aware of,

142
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
actually I'm pretty sure I would demand at least as high level,

143
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
if not a higher level of quality, from my sons.

144
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
So maybe that's where my hesitation is coming from.

145
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Anyway, should I just get over it and play the role of counselor for my sons,

146
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
or should I stay away from it all together?

147
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Well, Mike, thanks for getting in touch,

148
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
And the short answer is: get over it.

149
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,000
And the really long answer is: while you're in your dad role with your sons,

150
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,000
you're a dad.

151
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
While you're in your Marriott Badge Counselor role,

152
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
you're a Marriott Badge Counselor to a scout.

153
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
You're not a dad to your son.

154
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Now, your sons have two roles too- Scouts and Sons,

155
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,000
And they shouldn't feel like they have to occupy both those roles at once,

156
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
and neither should you.

157
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
If you want to counsel the badges for your son, go right ahead.

158
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Don't over or under, do anything,

159
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
And that can be harder than it sounds, I know.

160
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Just be clear about what role you are in and what role they are in

161
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
if you counsel a Marriott Badge for them

162
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:49,000
And then don't switch back and forth between roles while you're doing it

163
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
And I'll tell you with your sons. they probably get this anyway

164
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:57,000
and they'll give you one of those jeez dad eye rolls when you talk about this,

165
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
But it's a worthy conversation to have if you're going to counsel the badges with them.

166
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
As for a possible question at a border review,

167
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
anyone asking that question is saying a lot more about themselves

168
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
than the scout they're questioning.

169
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
There are a few people out there who are so wrapped around their own axle

170
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
they think they need to be inquisitors and defenders of the

171
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
sacred advancement requirements.

172
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:22,000
But I've kind of learned to ignore them and I never allow them anywhere near one of my scouts.

173
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,000
There is no policy against parents counseling Marriott Badges for their own sons

174
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and there is also no review process for Marriott Badges.

175
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
A counselor's signature is the end of the matter

176
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,000
and the question like the one that you think might come up at a border review

177
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,000
probably never should.

178
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
If the whole thing just really, really bugs you,

179
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
there's no harm in, no foul in telling your sons

180
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
that even though you could counsel the badges for them,

181
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:49,000
you'd just rather not because it just doesn't seem like a good idea to you.

182
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:53,000
I'm sure they will probably understand and so will everybody else.

183
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,000
Finally, I'd point out that if there is indeed a Marriott Badge, counselor

184
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:00,000
would fast track his own son for something like this.

185
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
they wouldn't even consider asking this question anyways.

186
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
The fact that it's on your radar means you're an honest, honorable person,

187
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
so give yourself a pat on the back.

188
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
Allison wrote in and said:

189
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:11,000
I had a question about blue cards.

190
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
Once we finish the Marriott Badge, do we tear them apart

191
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
and give them to the counselor or the scout in the advancement committee,

192
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
or are we supposed to keep them together?

193
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,000
We're having some disagreements about how we're supposed to handle this

194
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:22,000
and our truth.

195
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
Well, Allison, the best way to resolve those disagreements

196
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:29,000
is to make sure everyone has a copy of the Guide to Advancement 2013,

197
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
where the procedures and policies about blue cards are spelled out quite clearly.

198
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,000
It's freely available as a PDF document online and it's pretty easy to find.

199
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:42,000
The section on Marriott Badges contains a detailed description

200
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
of how the application for a Marriott Badge is administered,

201
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:49,000
and the short story is is that, as you suspect,

202
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
the counselor retains the counselor. third,

203
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
the scout holds on to the scout. third,

204
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
and the unit holds on to the unit. third.

205
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
That's why it's perforated,

206
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
So you can take those sections apart from one another.

207
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:07,000
George Zach is with Boy Scout Troops 767 in Broomfield, Colorado.

208
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
During the conversation we had recently,

209
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
one of my scouts was a little put off with the fact

210
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
that you could qualify for higher ranks

211
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:18,000
by being something like a librarian or a scribe or a historian,

212
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
because he didn't feel those were leadership roles,

213
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
because in those roles you more or less

214
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
are serving your fellow scouts

215
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
instead of actually giving them leadership.

216
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
I also hear a fair amount of grumbling

217
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
about these quote, lesser unquote positions

218
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
being applied to rank advancement.

219
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:37,000
I'd be curious what you have to say

220
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and I look forward to your input.

221
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Well, George, I'm going to make a very controversial,

222
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
shocking and completely true statement.

223
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
We aren't aimed at making leaders

224
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
or training leaders.

225
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:50,000
That's right. we aren't.

226
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
That's not our principal aim.

227
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
I'll agree that one of the many things that we do

228
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
for our scouts is give them the chance

229
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
to learn something about leadership

230
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,000
and develop the skills that are required of any leader,

231
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
but developing leaders is not our single aim.

232
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
I'll take the opportunity to say that we are,

233
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
to my mind at least,

234
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
way, way overboard and way too focused

235
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
on the whole leadership development paradigm

236
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
part of scouting.

237
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
We aren't aimed at producing leaders

238
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,000
to the exclusion of all else,

239
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and I think we get really side tracked

240
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
into that sometimes.

241
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
There are two or three important statements

242
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
of what we really are aimed at.

243
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
The first is the BSA mission statement

244
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
and it reads like this:

245
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,000
the mission of the Boy Scouts of America

246
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
is to prepare young people

247
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
to make ethical and moral choices

248
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
over their lifetimes

249
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
by instilling in them the values of the scout oath

250
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
and scout law.

251
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
And the second one is a statement

252
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
about the aims of scouting

253
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
from the Scout Master Handbook.

254
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Every scouting activity

255
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
moves boys towards three basic aims:

256
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
character development,

257
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
citizenship training

258
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
and mental and physical fitness.

259
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
Now look at those two statements.

260
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
they pretty well define

261
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
what the aim of our work is,

262
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
and you don't see leadership mentioned at all.

263
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
Making people into leaders

264
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
is actually really not all that difficult.

265
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
There are plenty of opportunities for that

266
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
in a young person's life.

267
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
I think if you read either statement

268
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
you'd agree that the virtues represented

269
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
by the scout oath and law

270
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
and all the things that we're aimed at

271
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
producing- people of good character

272
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
and good citizenship

273
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
and mental and physical fitness-

274
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
are all constituents of being a leader.

275
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
but there's a whole lot more going on, isn't there?

276
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Now imagine a vocational training program

277
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
that was aimed at producing

278
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
trained, competent carpenters right

279
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
If they spent 80% of their time

280
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
focused on perfecting swinging a hammer,

281
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
to the exclusion of other important things

282
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
about the job.

283
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
they'd produce really good hammer swingers,

284
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
but poor carpenters.

285
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Does a carpenter have to know

286
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
how to swing a hammer? Well, sure he does,

287
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
but that's just one piece of a bigger puzzle.

288
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Do we develop leadership skills

289
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
in scouting? Sure, we do,

290
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
but it's only one element

291
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
and it's not the end of the aim itself.

292
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
The whole matter, I think, is handled

293
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
very, very well and very succinctly

294
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
in the Guide to Advancement 2013,

295
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
and here's the section.

296
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
are you ready? You got your pencil ready?

297
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:22,000
It's section 4.2.3.4.6.

298
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
It really will help you find it.

299
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
and that section is titled

300
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
Responsibility and Leadership

301
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
and it reads thusly:

302
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
Many suggest this requirement

303
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
should call for a position

304
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
of leadership rather than simply

305
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
of responsibility.

306
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Taking an accepting responsibility,

307
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
however,

308
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
is a key foundation

309
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
for leadership.

310
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
One cannot lead effectively

311
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
without it.

312
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
The requirement, as written,

313
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
recognizes the different personalities,

314
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
talents and skill sets

315
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
in all of us.

316
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Some seem destined to be

317
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
the quote leader of the group,

318
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
unquote. Others

319
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
provide quality support

320
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
and strong examples behind the scenes.

321
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
Without the latter,

322
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
leaders in charge have little chance

323
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
for success. Thus,

324
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
the work of the supporters

325
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
becomes part of the overall

326
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
leadership effort.

327
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
So, even though it might sound a little heretical

328
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and it might give you pause,

329
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
I'm going to say that scouting

330
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
is not a school of leadership.

331
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
It's a movement that has much wider aims.

332
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
The arguments about this

333
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
usually end up circling around

334
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
when, and becoming the most

335
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
strident when, we're discussing

336
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
an Eagle Scout. If you take a little

337
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
time to review the requirements, you won't find

338
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
the word leadership mentioned except

339
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
in association with the Eagle Project,

340
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
which is designed for a

341
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
Scout to demonstrate his ability to lead

342
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and to serve others. Now, will our Scouts

343
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
go on to become leaders in life?

344
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
Yes, some of them will. Will they

345
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
be responsible, contributing citizens

346
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
who are able to make good

347
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
moral and ethical choices? Well,

348
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
that's really the entire aim

349
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
in scouting, isn't it? Our

350
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
entire aim is not

351
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
aimed at producing leaders.

352
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
So let's focus on the real aim of scouting

353
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
and not get ourselves

354
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
sidetracked into too many things.

355
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
So I've said all that to say this,

356
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
George: The practical

357
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
answer to the concern that was expressed to you

358
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
about the difference between

359
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
responsibility and leadership

360
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
is that we want people

361
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
to first be responsible,

362
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
to become good citizens

363
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
of good character.

364
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
That's the aim of scouting.

365
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
If they become leaders as a result

366
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
of that. that's also a very positive

367
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
outcome, isn't it? Our work

368
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
is focused on the

369
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
broader aim of scouting and not

370
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
at just one single aspect

371
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
of it. Listen, if you have a comment

372
